Group Report
1. Introduction

This project was completed over three sprints to design and implement a software system using architectural principles. The aim was to move from requirements to a working system while applying good design practices such as modularity, scalability, and clean architecture.

2. Sprint 1 – Requirements and Setup

In Sprint 1, we identified the system requirements and defined the project scope. These included both functional and non-functional requirements to ensure the system meets user needs.

We also set up the development environment using GitHub, created branches, and organized tasks using a project management tool. A Code of Conduct was agreed to ensure effective teamwork.

An initial component diagram was created to represent the system structure, and basic architectural elements were implemented in Java using abstract classes and interfaces.

3. Sprint 2 – Architecture Design

In Sprint 2, we developed the system architecture from the requirements.

We created:

A Business Concept Model to understand the domain
A Use Case Diagram to represent user interactions
System Interfaces to define system operations
A Business Type Model to structure key entities

These were combined into an initial system architecture, showing how components interact.

We applied Clean Architecture to separate concerns and improve maintainability. The system was implemented based on this structure using well-defined components and interfaces.

4. Sprint 3 – Implementation and Testing

In Sprint 3, we enhanced the system by dividing it into compound components and applying architectural styles such as:

Model-View-Controller (MVC)
Layered architecture

We also introduced principles of Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA) to improve modularity and interoperability.

The system was fully implemented and tested using defined test cases. Results confirmed that the system works as expected and meets the project requirements.

5. Teamwork and Contribution

Work was divided between Ahmed and Mostafa across all sprints. Tasks included requirements analysis, design, implementation, and testing. Both members contributed to coding, reviewing, and documentation.

The team collaborated using GitHub for version control and maintained consistent communication throughout the project.

6. Design Rationale

The system was designed with a focus on simplicity, scalability, and maintainability.

Clean Architecture was used to separate logic into layers, making the system easier to modify and test.
MVC and layered styles were applied to improve structure and reusability.
SOA principles were introduced to allow components to work independently and communicate efficiently.

These decisions ensure that the system can be extended in the future without major changes.

7. Conclusion

The project successfully progressed from requirements to a fully implemented system. By following structured architectural principles and maintaining good teamwork, the final system is well-organized, functional, and scalable.
